(a) There shall be no appeal from any decision or determination made by the building official in the enforcement or administration of this chapter. The decision or determination of the building official, acting upon advice of the city attorney and subject to the then existing supervisory authority, in any case in which the building official's judgment is questioned, shall be final.
(b) The building official shall be authorized to issue variances from the requirements of this chapter for the reconstruction, rehabilitation or restoration of structures listed on the National Register of Historic Places or the state inventory of historic places without regard to the procedure set forth in the remainder of this section; provided, however, that no variance shall be issued pursuant to this subsection unless the applicant for a variance furnishes satisfactory evidence in the form of copies of official documents certifying the listing of the structure as a historic place, and such evidence shall be maintained on file by the building official; and provided further, that no variance shall be issued pursuant to this subsection for an alteration or addition to a structure.
(c) Except as provided in subsection (b) of this section, no variance from the requirements of this chapter shall be granted unless authorized by the mayor and city council pursuant to the procedure set forth in subsections (d) through (m) of this section, which shall be strictly followed.
(d) Application for a variance shall be made in conjunction with the application for a development permit and shall be typewritten. Four copies of the application, including all information required herein, shall be filed with the building official, who shall forward one copy to the secretary of the commission and two copies to the city attorney.
(e) The applicant shall pay a nonrefundable fee upon filing the application, which shall be in addition to other charges routinely made for issuance of a development permit, to defray the city's administrative and other expenses incidental to consideration of the application for a variance. The amount of such fee shall be established by the mayor and city council, from time to time, and made available for public examination in the office of the city clerk.
(f) In addition to the information otherwise required in an application for a development permit, the application for a variance shall include the following:
(1) Site plan and building plan drawn to scale showing the nature, location, dimensions, and elevations of the area in question, existing or proposed structures, fill, storage of materials, drainage facilities, and the location of the foregoing;
(2) Area plan showing grade elevations and drainage facilities and elevations of structures on surrounding properties;
(3) Certification of the foregoing by a registered professional engineer or architect;
(4) A detailed statement showing that the applicant has good and sufficient cause for requesting a variance and that the applicant will suffer exceptional hardship should a variance be denied; and
(5) A detailed statement showing that a variance will not cause increased flood heights, additional threats to public safety or extraordinary public expense; create nuisance; cause fraud or a victimization of the public; or conflict with existing local laws or ordinances.
(g) The city attorney shall immediately forward one copy of the application for a variance, together with all supporting information, to the regional office of the federal emergency management agency and shall request the advice and comments of the agency regarding the application, which he shall report to the mayor and city council.
(h) The mayor and city council shall set a date for a public hearing on the application, which shall be held not less than 30 days thereafter, and the secretary of the commission shall cause notice of the date, time, place and purpose of the same, identifying the applicant and property in question, to be advertised in the newspaper in which official proceedings of the commission are published, once each week for four weeks preceding the hearing.
(i) In passing upon an application for a variance, the mayor and city council shall consider all technical evaluations, all relevant factors, all standards specified in other sections of this chapter and the following additional factors:
(1) Danger that materials may be swept onto other lands to the injury of others;
(2) Danger to life and property due to flooding or erosion damage;
(3) Susceptibility of the proposed facility and its contents to flood damage and the effect of such damage on the individual owner;
(4) Importance of the services to be provided by the proposed facility to the community;
(5) Necessity to the facility of a waterfront location, where applicable;
(6) Availability of alternate locations, not subject to flooding or erosion damage, for the proposed use;
(7) Compatibility of the proposed use with existing and anticipated development;
(8) Relationship of the proposed use to the comprehensive plan and floodplain management program for the area;
(9) Safety of access to the property in times of flood for ordinary and emergency vehicles;
(10) Expected heights, velocity, duration, rate of rise and sediment transport of the floodwaters and the effects of wave action, if applicable, expected at the site;
(11) Costs of providing governmental services during and after flood conditions, including maintenance and repair of public or quasi-public utilities and facilities such as sewer, gas, electrical and water systems, and streets and bridges;
(12) Any advice and comments received from the Federal Emergency Management Agency; and
(13) FEMA guidelines.
Editor's note— The Federal Emergency Management Agency's guidelines are not printed in this volume but are available in the city clerk's office.
(j) Upon consideration of the factors listed above and the purposes of this chapter, the mayor and city council may attach such conditions to the grant of a variance as it deems necessary or appropriate to further the purposes of this chapter.
(k) Variances shall not be granted except under the following conditions:
(1) A variance shall not be issued unless the alleged hardship is based on physical characteristics peculiar to the land in question. Characteristics pertaining to the proposed structure and personal characteristics of its inhabitants, the applicant or property owners, and economic or financial hardship will not be considered;
(2) A variance shall only be issued upon a determination that the variance is the minimum necessary, considering the flood hazard, to afford relief;
(3) A variance shall only be issued upon:
a. Showing of good and sufficient cause;
b. A determination that a failure to grant the variance would result in exceptional hardship to the applicant; and
c. A determination that the grant of a variance will not result in increased flood heights, additional threats to public safety, extraordinary public expense; create nuisance; cause fraud on or victimization of the public; or conflict with existing local laws or ordinances.
(l) The city clerk shall report the decision of the commission on the application for a variance to the building official, and the building official shall maintain a record of all requests for variances and the decisions of the commission thereon and shall report the same to the Federal Emergency Management Agency upon request.
(m) Any applicant to whom a variance is granted shall be given written notice specifying the difference between the base flood elevation and the elevation to which the structure is to be built and stating that the cost of flood insurance will be commensurate with the increased risk resulting from the reduced lowest floor elevation.
(Ord. No. 2008-08-01, § I(2-2), 8-28-2008)

